<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    DEVELOPER VERIFICATION TOOL — NOT PART OF THE PRODUCT. See tools/probes/README.md.

    Data Import — etap I0 (docs/design/data-import.md §6). Measures what an .xlsx ACTUALLY looks like to a
    reader, so the XlsxImportProvider (etap I9) is designed against facts:

      (G/X) a generated workbook carrying every trap: shared vs inline strings, a built-in date format, a
            custom date format, a boolean, a cached formula, an error cell, a row with a MISSING middle
            cell, and a row-index gap. What does each one look like via the cell API?
      (R)   is a streaming (SAX) read genuinely streaming? Contrast with the DOM read on a 100 000-row
            file — time and managed-heap delta (risk R8).
      (F)   the SAME questions against a workbook produced by real Excel (the user's own sample for this
            feature). A file generated by OpenXml is not evidence about what Excel writes. STRUCTURE ONLY —
            sheet names, dimension, cell kinds, number formats, a few sample cells.
      (D2)  does DocumentFormat.OpenXml refuse a legacy BIFF .xls? The premise of decision D2.

    Raw OpenXml only — no Core reference: at I0 no production import code exists.

    Deliberately NOT in EmberTern.slnx. Writes its scratch workbooks to C:\Temp and deletes them; it only
    ever READS the sample files, never writes to them.

    How to run: see the header of Program.cs.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="DocumentFormat.OpenXml" Version="3.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
